# Spartan Protocol & Gemtext Guide

Documentation > User Manual > Spartan Protocol & Gemtext Guide

LugalOS adopts Gemtext (text/gemini, .gmi) as its native lightweight hypertext format and Spartan (spartan://, port 300) as its hypermedia networking transport.

## Gemtext Format (.gmi)

Gemtext is a minimalist, line-oriented document format designed for clarity, deterministic parsing, and low resource overhead:

* Text: Standard paragraph text without inline styling tags.
* Headers: # Heading 1, ## Heading 2, ### Heading 3.
* Bullet Lists: Lines starting with * .
* Blockquotes: Lines starting with > .
* Preformatted Code: Enclosed within lines starting with ``` ` ```. Code lines are rendered in monospace 8x16 font without word wrapping.
* Hyperlinks: Lines starting with => URL [Optional Label]. Because all links occupy dedicated lines, linking is completely unambiguous.

## Browsing with gem and reader

LugalOS provides two complementary viewers for Gemtext documents:

1. Terminal Viewer (gem):
Operates in standard VT100/ANSI consoles and virtual terminals.
```
   lsh> gem /sd0/pub/help/shell.gmi
   lsh> gem spartan://spartan.mozz.us/
```
* Press 1 .. 9 to follow visible links.
* Press b to navigate back in history.
* Press Space / PageDown to scroll down.
* Press q to return to shell.

2. Ribbon Reader (reader):
Opens a dedicated window in the horizontal window ribbon with variable-pitch serif typography and touch navigation.
```
   lsh> reader /sd0/pub/help/spartan.gmi
```

## Spartan Protocol (spartan://)

Spartan is an ultra-lightweight internet hypermedia protocol designed to serve Gemtext content over plain TCP without cryptographic handshakes:
* Default Port: TCP port 300.
* Request Format:   [content_length]\r\n.
* Response Format:  \r\n.
* Status 2: Success (Gemtext streamed directly to client).
* Status 3: Redirect (client follows to new location).
* Status 4: Client error (e.g. Not Found or access denied).
* Status 5: Server error.

## The Sandboxed Spartan Server (spartand)

LugalOS includes a built-in Spartan server daemon that can serve local documents to the network:

```
lsh> spartan start        # Starts spartand on port 300
lsh> spartan status       # Displays daemon port and link status
lsh> spartan stop         # Stops spartand
```

### Security Discriminator & Sandbox Isolation

To protect system integrity while serving network requests:
* Chroot Jail: The daemon's document root is strictly bound to /sd0/pub/. Requests cannot access /sd0/system/, peer keys, or /proc/.
* Traversal Rejection: Any request containing .., backslashes, or null bytes is immediately rejected with status 40 Client Error.
* Static Content: Only regular files within /sd0/pub/ are served.

